{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1286 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "1c5d9498f6fbea599ca5fc4cf31907244b2d1f9f3c57161e7fac472e679d2218" :edb/db-before-t 1285 :edb/db-after-t 1286 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534598 50 #inst "2026-10-12T04:21:57.518Z" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186230296 1078 #edb/ref 17592186230301 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186231295 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t) = 4\\cos(4t)+12\\sin(4t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\n- $y = R\\cos(4t+\\phi) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\n- $y = R\\sin(4t+\\phi) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\n- $y = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $\\phi$ are constants." 13194139534598 17592186045417 false] [17592186231295 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t) = 4\\cos(4t)+12\\sin(4t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y = R\\cos(4t+\\phi) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\nII. $y = R\\sin(4t+\\phi) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\nIII. $y = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $\\phi$ are constants." 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186231295 1074 "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega.$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=4\\cos(4t)+12\\sin(4t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $4.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega = 4.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y_c = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi)} = R\\cos{(4t + \\phi)}$\n\n- $y_c = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi)} = R\\sin{(4t + \\phi)}$\n\n- $y_c =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t} = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega$ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(t)=t(C\\cos(4t)+D\\sin(4t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega=4$ into the differential equation, $y'' + \\omega^2y = f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[8(D-2Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8(C+2Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)]+16t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ 8D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-\\dfrac{3}{2}$ and $D=\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(t) = -\\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- $y = R\\cos{(4t + \\phi)} - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\n- $y = R\\sin{(4t + \\phi)} - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\n- $y = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\nOnly solutions I and II match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139534598 17592186045417 false] [17592186231295 1074 "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega.$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=4\\cos(4t)+12\\sin(4t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $4.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega = 4.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. $y_c = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi)} = R\\cos{(4t + \\phi)}$\n\n2\\. $y_c = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi)} = R\\sin{(4t + \\phi)}$\n\n3\\. $y_c =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t} = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega$ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(t)=t(C\\cos(4t)+D\\sin(4t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega=4$ into the differential equation, $y'' + \\omega^2y = f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[8(D-2Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8(C+2Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)]+16t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ 8D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-\\dfrac{3}{2}$ and $D=\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(t) = -\\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y = R\\cos{(4t + \\phi)} - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\n2\\. $y = R\\sin{(4t + \\phi)} - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\n3\\. $y = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\nOnly solutions I and II match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186231427 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427273 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186231427 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427274 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186231428 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427270 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186231428 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427271 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186231428 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427275 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186231429 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427272 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186231520 1050 1500.0 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186231571 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186231571 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t) = 3\\sin t$.\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)$?\n\n1. $y = R\\cos (t + ϕ) - \\frac{3}{2}t\\cos t$\n2. $y = R\\sin (t + ϕ) + 3t\\sin t$\n3. $y = A\\cos t + B\\sin t - 3t\\cos t + t\\sin t$\n\nNote that $A$, $B$, $R$, and $ϕ$ are constants." 13194139534598 17592186045417 false] [17592186231571 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)+3t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186231571 1074 "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $1.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =1.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =1$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(2D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-(2C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)]+t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2D\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $D=0.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+ϕ)-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$\n\nOnly solution I matches the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186231987 1064 "Consider an undamped forced oscillator,\n\n$$\ny''+\\omega^2y=f(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\omega$ is the system's natural frequency. Its general solution is\n\n$$\ny=y_c+y_p,\n$$\n\nwhere $y_c$ solves the homogeneous equation $y''+\\omega^2y=0$ and $y_p$ is any particular solution to the full equation.\n\nNotice that the complementary solution can be written in any of the following equivalent forms:\n\n- $y_c = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n- $y_c = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n- $y_c =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nNow, suppose the external force has the resonant form\n\n$$\nf(t)=a\\cos(\\omega t)+b\\sin(\\omega t),\n$$\n\nso that the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega.$\n\nA sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega$ would duplicate $y_c,$ so at resonance we multiply by $t$ and use a trial of the form\n\n$$\ny_p=t\\bigl(C\\cos(\\omega t)+D\\sin(\\omega t)\\bigr).\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$ and $D,$ we first compute derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{'} & =C\\operatorname{cos}(\\omega t)+D\\operatorname{sin}(\\omega t)+t(-C\\omega \\operatorname{sin}(\\omega t)+D\\omega \\operatorname{cos}(\\omega t)) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{''} & =2(-C\\omega \\operatorname{sin}(\\omega t)+D\\omega \\operatorname{cos}(\\omega t))-t{\\omega }^{2}(C\\operatorname{cos}(\\omega t)+D\\operatorname{sin}(\\omega t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the equation, we have that the $t$-terms cancel out, and we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{''}+{\\omega }^{2}{y}_{p}=2\\omega D\\operatorname{cos}(\\omega t)-2\\omega C\\operatorname{sin}(\\omega t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we need $y_p''+\\omega^2y_p=f(t)=a\\cos(\\omega t)+b\\sin(\\omega t),$ equating coefficients gives\n\n$$\n2\\omega D=a,\n\\qquad\n-2\\omega C=b.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nC=-\\frac{b}{2\\omega},\n\\qquad\nD=\\frac{a}{2\\omega},\n$$\n\nso a standard resonant particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p =\\frac{t}{2\\omega}\\bigl(-b\\cos(\\omega t)+a\\sin(\\omega t)\\bigr).\n$$\n\nSo, when $f(t)=a\\cos(\\omega t)+b\\sin(\\omega t)$ and resonance occurs, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y=A\\cos(\\omega t)+B\\sin(\\omega t)+\\dfrac{t}{2\\omega}\\bigl(-b\\cos(\\omega t)+a\\sin(\\omega t)\\bigr)}.\n$$" 13194139534598 17592186045417 false] [17592186231987 1064 "Consider an undamped forced oscillator,\n\n$$\ny''+\\omega^2y=f(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\omega$ is the system's natural frequency. Its general solution is\n\n$$\ny=y_c+y_p,\n$$\n\nwhere $y_c$ solves the homogeneous equation $y''+\\omega^2y=0$ and $y_p$ is any particular solution to the full equation.\n\nNotice that the complementary solution can be written in any of the following equivalent forms:\n\n1\\. $y_c = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n2\\. $y_c = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n3\\. $y_c =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nNow, suppose the external force has the resonant form\n\n$$\nf(t)=a\\cos(\\omega t)+b\\sin(\\omega t),\n$$\n\nso that the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega.$\n\nA sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega$ would duplicate $y_c,$ so at resonance we multiply by $t$ and use a trial of the form\n\n$$\ny_p=t\\bigl(C\\cos(\\omega t)+D\\sin(\\omega t)\\bigr).\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$ and $D,$ we first compute derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)+t(-C\\omega \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)+D\\omega \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & =2(-C\\omega \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)+D\\omega \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t))-t{\\omega }^{2}(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the equation, we have that the $t$-terms cancel out, and we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}{y}_{p}=2\\omega D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)-2\\omega C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we need $y_p''+\\omega^2y_p=f(t)=a\\cos(\\omega t)+b\\sin(\\omega t),$ equating coefficients gives\n\n$$\n2\\omega D=a,\n\\qquad\n-2\\omega C=b.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nC=-\\frac{b}{2\\omega},\n\\qquad\nD=\\frac{a}{2\\omega},\n$$\n\nso a standard resonant particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p =\\frac{t}{2\\omega}\\bigl(-b\\cos(\\omega t)+a\\sin(\\omega t)\\bigr).\n$$\n\nSo, when $f(t)=a\\cos(\\omega t)+b\\sin(\\omega t)$ and resonance occurs, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y=A\\cos(\\omega t)+B\\sin(\\omega t)+\\dfrac{t}{2\\omega}\\bigl(-b\\cos(\\omega t)+a\\sin(\\omega t)\\bigr)}.\n$$" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186317427 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." 13194139534598 17592186045417 false] [17592186317427 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186317427 1074 "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $2.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =2.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different for