17592186045498 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427263 1083 #uuid "e626483d-6393-5f32-a50e-826803c2da5c" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427264 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427247 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427264 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427248 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427264 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427255 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427264 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427257 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427264 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427259 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427264 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427264 1077 "selection" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427264 1078 #edb/ref 17592186427257 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427264 1079 #uuid "381db720-afce-51ba-af73-b0e9b580a593" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427265 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427253 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427265 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427265 1077 "field-1" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427265 1078 #edb/ref 17592186427253 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427265 1079 #uuid "69cfd4ec-f46a-5ece-a767-ad4f9e514f71" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427251 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427252 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427256 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427262 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427263 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427266 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427266 1077 "selection" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427266 1078 #edb/ref 17592186427262 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427266 1079 #uuid "6cc14bda-f7aa-549b-a048-07720db9b5c9" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427267 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427246 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427267 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427267 1077 "field-1" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427267 1078 #edb/ref 17592186427246 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427267 1079 #uuid "83cf42b6-01c4-5316-9134-17e65d43a4bd" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427268 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427258 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427268 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427268 1077 "field-1" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427268 1078 #edb/ref 17592186427258 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427268 1079 #uuid "96859c3d-ea02-5565-a7b1-fd4022be8699" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427269 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427249 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427269 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427250 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427269 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427254 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427269 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427260 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427269 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427261 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427269 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427269 1077 "selection" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427269 1078 #edb/ref 17592186427250 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427269 1079 #uuid "be790569-47e5-5c68-b35d-041a395703da" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427267 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427270 1069 "q-343697" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427270 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427270 1071 "A particle of mass $0.4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=2{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427270 1072 #uuid "0a402785-5a82-51a9-a9a5-3630928c9333" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427270 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.4{y}^{″}=-ky+2{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{2}y=5{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{2}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=5{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=5{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)=\\frac{5}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $5.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {5}^{2}=10\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427271 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427265 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427271 1069 "q-343685" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427271 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427271 1071 "A particle of mass $0.4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=-3sin\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427271 1072 #uuid "3803507d-5804-5835-967d-6fb55640e457" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427271 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.4{y}^{″}=-ky-3sin\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{2}y=-\\frac{15}{2}sin\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{2}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=-\\frac{15}{2}sin\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t),$ which is $\\frac{5}{2},$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427272 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427264 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427272 1069 "q-343753" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427272 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427272 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427272 1072 #uuid "528870e0-56ba-55b5-a9bc-4fec3b9615fa" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427272 1074 "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $3.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =3.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =3$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(6D-9Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-(6C+9Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)]+9t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 6D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-6C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-1$ and $D=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nOnly solution III matches the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427273 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427269 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427273 1069 "q-94237" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427273 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427273 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8y=4{cos}^{2}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427273 1072 #uuid "ae78c4ab-202a-5311-a7a5-9b6c9263c259" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427273 1074 "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=8\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\sqrt{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=4{cos}^{2}⁡pt$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =2\\sqrt{2}t.$ Since \n\n$$\nf(t)=4{cos}^{2}⁡pt=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2pt,\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $2p.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=\\frac{\\omega }{2}=\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427274 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427266 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427274 1069 "q-94234" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427274 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427274 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427274 1072 #uuid "dd36548a-cee9-58ad-bcce-622c4e2dff50" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427274 1074 "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=1\\,⟹\\,\\omega =1.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =1.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=1.$" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427268 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427275 1069 "q-343675" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427275 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427275 1071 "A particle of mass $0.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427275 1072 #uuid "ec4118d5-0fd0-57bc-bd76-5715be7cd077" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true] [17592186427275 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.5{y}^{″}=-ky-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2ky=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2k\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),$ which is $4,$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139534598 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-09370f9b-86b8-5add-b4ad-5915bb394165" 17592186427246 "answer-1d7998eb-7eaf-5245-8c62-e37c7ec23280" 17592186427247 "answer-25a494c8-57d1-53c2-87ed-c49b33af7999" 17592186427248 "answer-26077737-8ba6-52d8-a130-7bb98621fc19" 17592186427249 "answer-34b4e83a-d0e0-527d-a53e-c34aa3793a56" 17592186427250 "answer-3e4a1d9f-f5d4-5edb-b00d-96e2b58ea65b" 17592186427251 "answer-467b4288-3365-5b2b-bc4c-ec1682758746" 17592186427252 "answer-4e302561-84c6-577f-86fe-39f014a5ce75" 17592186427253 "answer-4f792fe5-ab17-5321-a1fb-b45187d74bee" 17592186427254 "answer-59b394d0-eddc-5f9f-8b4a-e200803eeb81" 17592186427255 "answer-70fbbf9f-3577-55db-8dae-d950e945e12d" 17592186427256 "answer-85e3b9f0-cb0f-56b7-9950-1757837f3c93" 17592186427257 "answer-8e3c6435-63de-52dc-a26e-ae48de64b25c" 17592186427258 "answer-95e627ca-5870-5885-9e85-231290fb62f9" 17592186427259 "answer-a2163383-f83a-5102-8354-9dd07eb8238d" 17592186427260 "answer-b5a30bf1-16e7-552c-abd3-762c6873fb4f" 17592186427261 "answer-cf95376e-910d-5172-858d-e0bb5102291d" 17592186427262 "answer-e626483d-6393-5f32-a50e-826803c2da5c" 17592186427263 "answer-field-381db720-afce-51ba-af73-b0e9b580a593" 17592186427264 "answer-field-69cfd4ec-f46a-5ece-a767-ad4f9e514f71" 17592186427265 "answer-field-6cc14bda-f7aa-549b-a048-07720db9b5c9" 17592186427266 "answer-field-83cf42b6-01c4-5316-9134-17e65d43a4bd" 17592186427267 "answer-field-96859c3d-ea02-5565-a7b1-fd4022be8699" 17592186427268 "answer-field-be790569-47e5-5c68-b35d-041a395703da" 17592186427269 "qu